  "body": "This is my first issue I've created against the spec... apologies for any breaches in protocol!\r\n\r\nI ran across some very odd behavior in the `<style>` tag that cost me almost a day of troubleshooting, and as far as I can tell (I haven't learned to read the HTML/DOM spec yet), it's part of the spec - all major browsers I tested have this behavior (Firefox, Chrome, Safari).\r\n\r\nEssentially, if you have a `<style>` tag, and use `sheet.insertRule` to add some rules, remove the element from the document, then add it back, it comes back without any of the dynamically added styles. This is very confusing and surprising where Custom Elements/Shadow DOM are involved, because they often contain styles intended to be scoped to that element, and it's quite reasonable to remove a custom element and then re-add it somewhere.\r\n\r\nHere is a CodeSandbox showing the issue: https://codesandbox.io/s/shadow-dom-bug-bqcce?file=/src/index.js\r\n\r\nI would expect the stylesheet to be maintained within the Shadow DOM, even if it's not currently connected to the document. Or maybe it could be recreated when added back to the document? I'm not really sure how all this works under the hood, I just know that it's very surprising behavior from a developer's perspective.",
